9 #uuid "a911c19c-a198-57f8-8ad5-1e251d33cc93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534955 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534955 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534955 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534955 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534955 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534955 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534955 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534955 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186534726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534955 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "de37fba6-41ca-540b-a8fd-47ec845611d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534983 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534983 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161328" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534983 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534983 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/78/7836860694ae6246dee36a70b86bc7fcd8cf7358d47a10586ea6880759fb1588.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=4x+\\sqrt{8}y$ and whose domain is the circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534983 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1bae08c0-8385-58de-b3f9-2f81e17da99b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534983 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=\\sqrt{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{4}^{2}+(\\sqrt{8})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{80\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535047 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535047 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161354" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535047 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535047 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z={e}^{x}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $x\\le y\\le x+1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535047 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f471502-6ac3-5895-874a-d7c88c5897e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535047 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{z}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}+{0}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{x+1}{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}y]}_{y=x}^{y=x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}(1+{e}^{2x})^{3/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((1+{e}^{2})^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535075 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535075 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161350" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535075 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535075 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{z}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=y-3x$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $3x\\le y\\le 3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535075 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e16a799c-84bb-5d3d-b669-6d70bdf08db4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535075 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-3x)=-3,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-3x)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{z}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{y-3x}\\sqrt{1+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{y-3x}\\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{y-3x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{3x}^{3}\\sqrt{y-3x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{2}{3}(y-3x)^{3/2}]}_{y=3x}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3-3x)^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{3}\\,{[\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})(3-3x)^{5/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{11}}{45}(0-{3}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{11}}{45}(9\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{33}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535084 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535084 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161327" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535084 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535084 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x+\\sqrt{2}y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535084 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f409435d-6032-5802-bf1f-c20613731f7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535084 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+\\sqrt{2}y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+\\sqrt{2}y)=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(1)}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2(x+\\sqrt{2}y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\,2({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}xy)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
